#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e25ce9f-c93f-5dc0-9e81-2093663c6202" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decimal Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0255ecbf-c1db-5dd4-9704-e600db5a7b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.1$ and $4.2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557874} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc973d7-9ef7-55a8-aa15-5cebae8878da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.2$ and $4.3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558107} {:answer/id #uuid "a84929bf-6e97-5748-a943-0a0b5e05fe47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.4$ and $4.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558200} {:answer/id #uuid "ad826c42-976c-512d-9b2e-79b932497737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.3$ and $4.4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558213} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b24de2-6f5d-5729-8cc4-26f97de70710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.6$ and $2.7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad826c42-976c-512d-9b2e-79b932497737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.3$ and $4.4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558213} :answer-field/id #uuid "b955f19e-ba63-5771-b01f-46068f7acf05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d76dfed4-6285-5a3d-a934-894e3d9dca0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-556448" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt[3]{80}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{80}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $80$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{80}$ lies.\nWe know that ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125$.\nSince $64<80<125$, we know that $4<\\sqrt[3]{80}<5$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $4$ and $5$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4.2}^{3} & =74.088 \\\\ {4.3}^{3} & =79.507 \\\\ {4.4}^{3} & =85.184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $79.507<80<85.184$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{4.3}^{3}<80<{4.4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n4.3<\\sqrt[3]{80}<4.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{80}$ lies between $4.3$ and $4.4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering a Set of Irrational Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a790d9-9438-5392-9289-b1003685cdba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{23},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557951} {:answer/id #uuid "3766ddbe-4bf0-51e9-969a-a99d4751a660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{23},$ $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557979} {:answer/id #uuid "77ba1fb3-5cca-58d6-9256-74955648cfba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{23}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558100} {:answer/id #uuid "be26cfba-432c-57a0-9aac-b8a808880469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{23},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558246} {:answer/id #uuid "e8bfaba7-4a0d-535f-b95d-5d514f95ac63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{23}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77ba1fb3-5cca-58d6-9256-74955648cfba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{23}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558100} :answer-field/id #uuid "53107ea8-10ba-5146-bdcd-b91afbcf0d1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60620631-f27c-5865-a3ad-1d8af90c2532" :question/math-academy-id "q-556454" :question/problem "Which of the following lists $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ and $\\sqrt{23}$ in order from least to greatest?" :question/worked-solution "To order the irrational numbers $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt{23}$ from least to greatest, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each square root lies.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{3},$ the closest perfect squares are $1$ and $4.$ Since $1<3<4,$ we have $\\sqrt{1}<\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{4},$ which means $1<\\sqrt{3}<2.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7},$ the closest perfect squares are $4$ and $9.$ Since $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9},$ which means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{23},$ the closest perfect squares are $16$ and $25.$ Since $16<23<25,$ we have $\\sqrt{16}<\\sqrt{23}<\\sqrt{25},$ which means $4<\\sqrt{23}<5.$\n\nComparing these bounds, we can see that the intervals do not overlap and fall into increasing ranges. This gives the order\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{23}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the list in order from least to greatest is $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{23}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering a Set of Irrational Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c6f251-d57d-5887-ac6b-c4f33edcc1e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{10},$ $\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{29}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557938} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7f2e96-98ad-5f9b-a510-db810bf8a345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{10},$ $\\sqrt{29},$ $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557987} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7f93df-6616-570a-b072-00e563bd75e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{29},$ $\\sqrt{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558041} {:answer/id #uuid "62f8a429-52b2-5395-b288-300f26d03410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{29},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558056} {:answer/id #uuid "a9fb6f09-fdc8-50e6-b565-de21cfd7ee05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ $\\sqrt{29}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9fb6f09-fdc8-50e6-b565-de21cfd7ee05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ $\\sqrt{29}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558202} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce2d1112-d79c-5535-b77b-b1cd4861b280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3b24341-9377-52eb-a66f-7900d0e4a928" :question/math-academy-id "q-556455" :question/problem "Which of the following lists $\\sqrt{29},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ and $\\sqrt{6}$ in order from least to greatest?" :question/worked-solution "To order the irrational numbers $\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ and $\\sqrt{29}$ from least to greatest, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each square root lies.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{6},$ the closest perfect squares are $4$ and $9.$ Since $4<6<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{6}<\\sqrt{9},$ which means $2<\\sqrt{6}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{10},$ the closest perfect squares are $9$ and $16.$ Since $9<10<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{10}<\\sqrt{16},$ which means $3<\\sqrt{10}<4.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{29},$ the closest perfect squares are $25$ and $36.$ Since $25<29<36,$ we have $\\sqrt{25}<\\sqrt{29}<\\sqrt{36},$ which means $5<\\sqrt{29}<6.$\n\nComparing these bounds, we can see that the intervals do not overlap and fall into increasing ranges. This gives the order\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}<\\sqrt{10}<\\sqrt{29}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the list in order from least to greatest is $\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ $\\sqrt{29}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f6b9f6c-95f0-5c70-b22d-f60c0e9d20da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Unit Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287ef716-a2c9-568c-bdfd-0f4d012e579b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$26$ and $28$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557948} {:answer/id #uuid "64b90d65-f33c-5c11-b9b5-16257b1b6252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558063} {:answer/id #uuid "b62071b6-2f8a-5595-ad60-1d22fac5f4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558231} {:answer/id #uuid "ca560bb5-28ca-5e3a-b1d8-05d86eca0dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558271} {:answer/id #uuid "d62e4a9e-9adb-5933-850c-d68382286d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$27$ and $28$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca560bb5-28ca-5e3a-b1d8-05d86eca0dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558271} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff8d26b-d2d9-5237-a8b5-3d3f8b17bc68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8c35ff1-30ac-5b47-a7ed-d4cc77e1a1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-556460" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt{55}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{55}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $55.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $55$ are $49$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n49<55<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{49} & <\\sqrt{55}<\\sqrt{64} \\\\ 7 & <\\sqrt{55}<8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/71bc6c197bd0fb3444a7105d8da77ead56cde1da0bb76139668778bfb4b3cd22.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{55}$ lies between $7$ and $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83d6ac5f-99e4-5420-9047-1c43a50b43a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fa08266-222c-5daf-a225-4d6ebf416ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fa08266-222c-5daf-a225-4d6ebf416ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558087} :answer-field/id #uuid "da3c162f-11dc-5f98-8e5a-a200564ea1fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ce3eb2d-c05e-546e-b2ae-aed79d4b89b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-556464" :question/problem "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt{45},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt{45}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate $\\sqrt{45},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $45.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $36={6}^{2}$ and $49={7}^{2}.$\n\nSince $36<45<49,$ we know that the square root of $45$ lies between $6$ and $7.$\n\n![](images/f9/f9d2536ecb1a4d163997e6415b21ff95094074c7fb521a535fc41d33bb0f0173.png)\n\nSince $45$ lies between $36$ and $49,$ we expect $\\sqrt{45}$ to lie between $6$ and $7.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6.7}^{2} & =44.89 \\\\ {6.8}^{2} & =46.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $45$ lies between $44.89$ and $46.24,$ so $6.7<\\sqrt{45}<6.8.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $45:$\n\n- The distance from $45$ to ${6.7}^{2}$ is $45-44.89=0.11.$\n\n- The distance from $45$ to ${6.8}^{2}$ is $46.24-45=1.24.$\n\nSince $45$ is much closer to $44.89,$ $\\sqrt{45}$ is closer to $6.7.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{45}≈6.7.$"}]}
